{"input_text": "Question: So I need to calculate 
\begin{equation*}
\int \frac{\exp(z) \sin(3z)}{(z^2-2)z^2} \, dz~\text{on}~|z|=1. 
\end{equation*}
So I have found the singularities and residues and observed that the singularities $2^{1/2}$, $-2^{1/2}$ (the other singularity is $0$) lie outside of the radius we are looking at. My thoughts were to split in to partial fractions and the integral of the fractions corresponding with these singularities would be zero by Cauchy-Goursat theorem. But I'm unsure how to proceed with the partial fractions as my attempts keep failing. Is this even the right approach? 
 Thanks! 

Answer: There is a pole of order one at $z=0$.  So, by the residue theorem, that residue is given by  
 $$\lim_{z \to 0}z\frac{e^{z}\sin(3z)}{(z^2-2)z^2}=-\frac{3}{2}$$ 
