{"input_text": "Question: A parallelogram P in the $xy$-plane has vertex $(0,0)$, $(2, 10)$, $(3, 17)$, and 
$(1, 7)$. 
 I need to find a linear transformation that maps P onto a rectangle $R$ in the $uv$-plane with opposite vertices $(0,0)$ and $(4,2)$ in order to calculate a related integral. 
 I can't seem to find a correct linear transformation. What could I try? Is there a procedure that can be applied in this sort of scenarios? 

Answer: Your linear transformation is a $2\times 2$ matrix $A$ that satisfies
\begin{align*}
A\begin{bmatrix}2\\10\end{bmatrix} 
&= \begin{bmatrix}4\\0\end{bmatrix} &
A\begin{bmatrix}1\\7\end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 2\end{bmatrix} &
A\begin{bmatrix}3\\17\end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix}4\\2\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
Note, however, that the third equation is redundant since the first two equations imply
$$
A\begin{bmatrix}3\\17\end{bmatrix}
= A\left(
\begin{bmatrix}2\\10\end{bmatrix}+
\begin{bmatrix}1\\7\end{bmatrix}
\right)
=A\begin{bmatrix}2\\10\end{bmatrix}+
A\begin{bmatrix}1\\7\end{bmatrix}
=\begin{bmatrix}4\\0\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}0\\2\end{bmatrix}
=\begin{bmatrix}4\\2\end{bmatrix}
$$
Thus we need only find a $2\times 2$ matrix satisfying
\begin{align*}
A\begin{bmatrix}2\\10\end{bmatrix} 
&= \begin{bmatrix}4\\0\end{bmatrix} &
A\begin{bmatrix}1\\7\end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 2\end{bmatrix} 
\end{align*}
But this is equivalent to finding a $2\times 2$ matrix satisfying
$$
A\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\10&7\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}4&0\\0&2\end{bmatrix}
$$
If we multiply both sides of this equation on the right by
$$
\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\10&7\end{bmatrix}^{-1}
$$
we obtain
$$
A=\begin{bmatrix}4&0\\0&2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\10&7\end{bmatrix}^{-1}
$$
Can you invert the matrix and carry out the multiplication to find $A$? 
 Of course, once we have our matrix
$$
A=\begin{bmatrix}a& b\\ c&d\end{bmatrix}
$$
our answer is
$$
A\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}ax+by\\ cx+dy\end{bmatrix}
$$ 
